# Schoolera core rules

## Before coding

- Read `docs/implementation-baseline.md` and `docs/localization.md` before implementing a feature.
- Inspect existing code before changing it.
- Complete only the requested prompt scope and stop.

## Architecture

- Preserve Clean Architecture projects: Api, Application, Domain, Infrastructure, Tests.
- Keep controllers thin; use MediatR (CQRS).
- Use explicit API DTOs; never expose EF entities from controllers.
- Preserve the `Result<T>` (`succeeded` / `data` / `errors` / `errorCodes`) response pattern.
- Preserve migrations and existing data; prefer additive schema changes.
- Do not create duplicate architectures, unnecessary generic repositories, microservices, event buses, or excessive abstractions.

## NSwag (official Angular API contract)

- NSwag is the official Angular API-contract generator.
- Generated file: `src/Schoolera.Api/Schoolera-SPA/src/app/core/api-client/SwaggerClient.service.ts`
- Never manually edit the generated file.
- Backend DTOs and OpenAPI are the source of truth.
- When contracts change: update backend → regenerate NSwag client → fix consuming Angular code.
- Wrap generated clients behind Angular feature services (for example `SchoolsApi`).
- Angular components must not normally depend on generated clients directly.
- Do not hand-write duplicates of generated API DTOs.
- UI-only view models are allowed for display, form state, filters, and non-API flags.

## Hosting and HTTP

- Preserve same-origin production `/api` behavior (NSwag paths are `/api/...`; base URL is usually empty).
- Never hardcode localhost addresses in production or generated client code.
- Prefer Angular proxy for Development relative `/api` requests.
- Preserve Swagger and SPA endpoint ordering: `MapControllers()` before `MapFallbackToFile("index.html")`.
- Unmatched `/api/**`, `/swagger/**`, and `/uploads/**` must return API/file 404, not Angular `index.html`.
- Use the global `Accept-Language` interceptor; do not set language headers in feature services.

## Angular UI

- Reusable shared components only when reused or part of the design system.
- Prefer extending existing feature-service and layout patterns.

## Localization (permanent — overrides older “English optional” wording)

- Arabic (`ar`, locale `ar-EG`, RTL) and English (`en`, locale `en-US`, LTR) are mandatory from Phase 1.
- Arabic is the default and fallback language.
- Read `docs/localization.md` before frontend or API-contract work.
- Use only the existing Transloco architecture (no ngx-translate, no Angular compile-time i18n second system).
- Do not hardcode user-facing UI strings; use translation keys in `public/i18n`.
- Do not duplicate page components per language.
- Static UI uses Transloco keys; managed public content uses explicit Arabic/English database fields when required.
- Management forms show both language fields (`se-bilingual-field-group`); public APIs may return localized display DTOs; management APIs return both language values.
- Localization validation (`npm run i18n:check` / `i18n:validate`) must pass before a feature is complete.
- Frontends branch on `errorCodes`, never on localized `errors` text.

## Authentication (Phase 1)

- ASP.NET Core Identity with HttpOnly application cookie (`ConfigureApplicationCookie`); no tokens in browser storage.
- Public registration creates only **Parent** and **SchoolOwner** roles.
- Centralized roles/policies in `Schoolera.Application.Auth.Constants`.
- State-changing cookie-authenticated `/api/**` POST requests require CSRF (`XSRF-TOKEN` / `X-XSRF-TOKEN`).
- API `401`/`403` return JSON `Result<T>` with stable `auth.*` error codes.
- See `docs/authentication.md` before auth work.

## Verification

- Run relevant backend and Angular builds/tests for the change.
- Never claim a command succeeded unless it was actually executed.
- Report real failures (Node version, SQL, etc.) honestly.
- Do not put machine-specific build failures into permanent rules.
